## Without a graph

Run `python3 scripts/map/areas.py`. It groups code files by folder, picks the symbols the rest of the code mentions most, and links the Markdown docs that name each folder. Commit the new areas.md.

## Optional: the graph runtime

graphify builds a richer graph of calls and imports. Use it only through a runtime that belongs to this project: a virtual environment under the gitignored `.local/tools/`, installed from hash-locked files. Check that `.local/` and `graphify-out/` are ignored before you build: `git check-ignore -v .local/ graphify-out/` must print a rule for each (an adopted project may keep its own .gitignore without them). Never install graphify globally, never use a `/graphify` skill or a global `graphify` command, and never let another project's hooks run it here.

One time setup, from the repository root. Replace `X.Y.Z` with the version your vetting log approves, and check the wheel hash against that entry.

```sh
python3 -m venv .local/tools/graphify-X.Y.Z
.local/tools/graphify-X.Y.Z/bin/python -m pip download graphifyy==X.Y.Z --only-binary=:all: -d .local/tools/wheels-X.Y.Z
shasum -a 256 .local/tools/wheels-X.Y.Z/graphifyy-X.Y.Z-*.whl
python3 scripts/map/graph.py lock .local/tools/wheels-X.Y.Z
.local/tools/graphify-X.Y.Z/bin/python -m pip install --no-index --no-deps --require-hashes --find-links .local/tools/wheels-X.Y.Z -r scripts/map/requirements.txt
.local/tools/graphify-X.Y.Z/bin/python -m pip check
```

1. `pip download` is the only step that uses the network. Stop if the graphifyy hash differs from your vetting log.
2. `graph.py lock` writes every downloaded wheel with its SHA-256 into requirements.txt. The lock is for this computer's platform and Python version; another platform downloads and locks its own.
3. The install is offline and refuses any file whose hash differs. Run a vulnerability audit, such as pip-audit from its own separate environment, before the first build.

Each rebuild:

```sh
.local/tools/graphify-X.Y.Z/bin/python -I scripts/map/graph.py build
python3 scripts/map/areas.py
```

`build` refuses to run from any interpreter other than the pinned one started with `-I`, or when graphify would load from outside that environment. It blocks network connections for the whole run, including graphify's worker processes, does not follow symlinks and skips `.local/`, `.git/` and generated output. It writes `graphify-out/graph.json` and `graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md`. Then areas.py rewrites areas.md from that graph.

## Optional: refresh after each commit

The hook is off by default. To turn it on for your clone:

```sh
hooks=$(git rev-parse --git-path hooks)
cp scripts/map/post-commit.sample "$hooks/post-commit"
chmod +x "$hooks/post-commit"
```

It runs only when the pinned runtime exists, in the background, so it never blocks or slows a commit, and it never touches the network. Its log is `.local/map-refresh.log`; a lock older than 30 minutes, left by a refresh that died, is cleared on the next commit. The hook has been tested on macOS only. It rebuilds the local graph and areas.md but does not commit them: review and commit areas.md yourself. Remove the hook file to turn it off. If the repository already has a post-commit hook or a hook manager, add the same lines to that instead.

## Limits

- Areas are groups of related code found by the graph or by folder, not modules someone designed. Their names come from their main folder and files.
- A plain scan finds definitions by pattern, so it misses some symbols and may count a common word as a mention.
- areas.md describes the commit named in its header. After large merges, rebuild it before trusting a file list.
